TinyUSB 0.21.0, DWC2 device: re-arm an isochronous OUT endpoint for the
(micro)frame after the packet it received, not the one after the re-arm.

The core receives an isochronous OUT packet only in a (micro)frame whose
parity is the endpoint's DOEPCTL.EONUM bit (RM0431 Rev 4 32.16.6, RM0456
Rev 7 73.15.6), and edpt_schedule_packets() sets that parity from DSTS.FNSOF
at the time of the re-arm: the (micro)frame after the current one. With one
transfer of one packet at a time, the endpoint is re-armed from the
transfer-complete interrupt of each packet. When a packet completes late in
its microframe P and the re-arm runs into P+1, today's rule targets P+2, so
the packet of P+1 is lost even when its token has not come yet.

At an isochronous OUT transfer complete, the driver now notes P from FNSOF
and EONUM (P = fn - ((fn ^ EONUM) & 1), since the completion is handled in
P or P+1), and the next re-arm targets the parity of P + interval as long as
that (micro)frame has not passed ((now - P) masked by speed <= interval);
otherwise, and for every other transfer, the rule is as before. The note is
used once, and is cleared when the endpoint is activated; a bus reset clears
it with the rest of xfer_status. A re-arm that lands after the token of P+1
then waits for P+3 and loses 2 packets instead of 1: measured, that share of
the late re-arms was about 1 in 170 on Linux at 705.6 and 768 kHz.

Applied by firmware/CMakeLists.txt (BPC_TUSB_PATCHES iso_out) to a copy in
the build folder; the submodule stays at the release.

